{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CRT69CQTdrAbHMHHwVCHqk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The _Black_ code style\n2\t\n3\t## Code style\n4\t\n5\t_Black_ aims for consistency, generality, readability and reducing git diffs. Similar\n6\tlanguage constructs are formatted with similar rules. Style configuration options are\n7\tdeliberately limited and rarely added. Previous formatting is taken into account as\n8\tlittle as possible, with rare exceptions like the magic trailing comma. The coding style\n9\tused by _Black_ can be viewed as a strict subset of PEP 8.\n10\t\n11\tThis document describes the current formatting style. If you're interested in trying out\n12\twhere the style is heading, see [future style](./future_style.md) and try running\n13\t`black --preview`.\n14\t\n15\t### How _Black_ wraps lines\n16\t\n17\t_Black_ ignores previous formatting and applies uniform horizontal and vertical\n18\twhitespace to your code. The rules for horizontal whitespace can be summarized as: do\n19\twhatever makes `pycodestyle` happy.\n20\t\n21\tAs for vertical whitespace, _Black_ tries to render one full expression or simple\n22\tstatement per line. If this fits the allotted line length, great.\n23\t\n24\t```py3\n25\t# in:\n26\t\n27\tj = [1,\n28\t     2,\n29\t     3\n30\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f190601d-a381-44c3-9103-79e71260c370","uuid":"582b8506-ae6c-4f08-8843-618e9793c61a","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:42:26.825Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/runner/work/black-triage-sandbox/black-triage-sandbox/docs/the_black_code_style/current_style.md","content":"# The _Black_ code style\n\n## Code style\n\n_Black_ aims for consistency, generality, readability and reducing git diffs. Similar\nlanguage constructs are formatted with similar rules. Style configuration options are\ndeliberately limited and rarely added. Previous formatting is taken into account as\nlittle as possible, with rare exceptions like the magic trailing comma. The coding style\nused by _Black_ can be viewed as a strict subset of PEP 8.\n\nThis document describes the current formatting style. If you're interested in trying out\nwhere the style is heading, see [future style](./future_style.md) and try running\n`black --preview`.\n\n### How _Black_ wraps lines\n\n_Black_ ignores previous formatting and applies uniform horizontal and vertical\nwhitespace to your code. The rules for horizontal whitespace can be summarized as: do\nwhatever makes `pycodestyle` happy.\n\nAs for vertical whitespace, _Black_ tries to render one full expression or simple\nstatement per line. If this fits the allotted line length, great.\n\n```py3\n# in:\n\nj = [1,\n     2,\n     3\n]","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":505}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CRT69CQTdrAbHMHHwVCHqk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
